+# Case Folding for the Specialty Match Happens in the Query
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+`REQ-VETSSPECIALTYFILTER-001` matches a specialty on its whole name, ignoring letter case. The three vendor schemas store the specialty name differently — H2 and MySQL as a bounded character column, PostgreSQL as text. H2 and PostgreSQL compare case-sensitively; MySQL's default collation does not. Case-insensitivity therefore cannot be inherited from the column.
+
+Owner search already took the other road. It leans on column type and collation, and the result is a confirmed defect: the same search returns different owners per vendor, breaching `REQ-OWN-002`.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Rely on column collation** — type the H2 column case-insensitively and lower-case in a PostgreSQL index, as pet-name uniqueness does. Rejected: three hand-maintained schemas would have to keep agreeing, and owner search shows what one drifting costs.
+2. **Fold case in the controller** — read every veterinarian and compare in Java. Rejected: selection is the repository's work, and an in-memory compare cannot be paged.
+3. **Fold case in the query** (chosen).
+
+## Decision
+
+The specialty match folds case inside the repository query, through the derived-query case-insensitive keyword. The generated SQL folds both sides of the comparison. No vendor schema changes.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- The filter selects the same veterinarians on all three vendors by construction, rather than by three schemas agreeing.
+- The index on the specialty name cannot serve the predicate, because the comparison is over an expression. The seeded specialty list is three rows, so the scan is not material.
+- Owner search keeps its collation-based matching and its defect. This ADR sets the pattern for new matching; it does not repair the old one.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Requirements:** REQ-VETSSPECIALTYFILTER-001
+
+## References
+
+- [system-design.md § Persistence](../system-design.md#persistence) — the rule this decision records
+- [system-design.md § Known Defects](../system-design.md#known-defects) — the vendor divergence in owner search
