+# Specialty-Filtered Veterinarian Reads Bypass the Vets Cache
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+Both existing veterinarian read methods are cached under a single `vets` cache. That cache is created programmatically from a bare JCache configuration, whose own source comment records that a size limit must come from the provider. No provider configuration is committed, so the cache is unbounded and evicts nothing.
+
+Its key space is closed today. One method takes no argument, the other takes a page request, and the data bounds both.
+
+`REQ-VETSSPECIALTYFILTER-001` adds a read keyed on a specialty name taken verbatim from a query parameter. Every route is open to every caller, so that text is unauthenticated caller input.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Cache the filtered reads alongside the others** — consistent with the sibling methods. Rejected: the key would derive from caller text, letting any caller insert entries into an unbounded cache without limit.
+2. **Bound the cache first, then cache the filtered reads.** Rejected for this slice: whether veterinarian data changes out of band is an open question, and sizing the cache is a decision of its own.
+3. **Leave the filtered reads uncached** (chosen).
+
+## Decision
+
+The specialty-filtered repository methods carry no cache annotation. The two unfiltered methods keep theirs, so the unnarrowed directory is served exactly as before. No cache key derives from request text while the cache is unbounded.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Every filtered request reaches the database. The veterinarian tables hold a handful of rows, so the cost is a small scan.
+- The `vets` cache key space stays closed and bounded by the data, as it is today.
+- Caching the filtered reads becomes available once the cache carries a size bound. This ADR defers to that open question rather than answering it.
+- The unfiltered and filtered paths differ in cache behavior, which a reader will notice. This ADR is the reason.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Requirements:** REQ-VETSSPECIALTYFILTER-001
+
+## References
+
+- [system-design.md § Persistence](../system-design.md#persistence) — the rule this decision records
+- [system-design.md § Open Questions from the Survey](../system-design.md#open-questions-from-the-survey) — vet cache invalidation and sizing
+- [security-principles.md § Realization](../security-principles.md#realization) — the baseline a change may not weaken
